C:\DocumentNoSnc\CC\GXEMUL\GXEMUL-SEC\regress\*.py
```

**Do not read `regress/artifacts/`, `tools/pipeline/ledger.json`, or `CHANGELOG.md`.** If you cite
anything outside `GXEMUL-SEC`, use an absolute path — a recent run of another brief named a file by a
path that did not resolve from its working directory, went looking for it, and spent its whole budget
enumerating an archive directory instead of answering.

Ordinary test-coverage question about an open-source CPU emulator written in C99. Three unrelated
2021-vintage copies of `src/` exist elsewhere on this machine and would give line numbers off by
hundreds; the tree under review is the one above.

**Please check the premises rather than confirming them** — a recent review here had seven of nine
readers confirm a premise stated wrongly.

## §1 — THE SITE, and the whole enclosing function is inlined because you may be asked about any arm

`src/cpus/cpu_arm_coproc.c`, `arm_coproc_i80321_6()` — Intel 80321 coprocessor 6:

```c
void arm_coproc_i80321_6(struct cpu *cpu, int opcode1, int opcode2, int l_bit,
	int crn, int crm, int rd)
{
	switch (crm) {

	case 0:	switch (crn) {
		case 0:	if (l_bit) {
				cpu->cd.arm.r[rd] = cpu->cd.arm.i80321_inten;
				fatal("TODO: XScale read from inten?\n");
				exit(1);
			} else
				cpu->cd.arm.i80321_inten = cpu->cd.arm.r[rd];
			break;
		case 4:	if (l_bit)
				cpu->cd.arm.r[rd] = cpu->cd.arm.i80321_isteer;
			else {
				cpu->cd.arm.i80321_isteer = cpu->cd.arm.r[rd];
				if (cpu->cd.arm.r[rd] != 0) {
					fatal("ARM xscale interrupt steering"
					    " is not yet implemented\n");
					exit(1);
				}
			}
			break;
		case 8:	if (l_bit)
				cpu->cd.arm.r[rd] = cpu->cd.arm.i80321_isrc;
			else {
				cpu->cd.arm.i80321_isrc = cpu->cd.arm.r[rd];
				fatal("TODO: XScale int ack?\n");
				exit(1);
			}
			break;
		default:goto unknown;
		}
		break;

	case 1:	/*  timers -- crn 0..7: tmr0, tmr1, tcr0, tcr1, trr0, trr1, tisr, wdtcr.
	           All fourteen read and write arms are quiet; two of them increment a
	           counter on read as a deliberate hack.  Elided here for length; ask me
	           and I will inline them verbatim.  */
		...
		default:goto unknown;
		}
		break;

	default:goto unknown;
	}

	return;

unknown:
	fatal("arm_coproc_i80321_6: unimplemented opcode1=%i opcode2=%i crn="
	    "%i crm=%i rd=%i l=%i)\n", opcode1, opcode2, crn, crm, rd, l_bit);
	exit(1);
}
```

**The proposed change is to delete the two lines `fatal(...)` and `exit(1)` from `crm 0, crn 0`'s read
arm**, leaving the assignment, with no diagnostic added: the register is modelled in both directions,
so a message saying the read is unimplemented would be a false statement in product code.

A design review has already ratified that disposition. It also corrected an overclaim of mine: I had
said the adjacent `case 4` "carries no terminating call", which is false of the whole case — its
*write* arm ends the process on any nonzero value. **The asymmetry that survives is in the read
direction only**: `crn 0` reads and dies, `crn 4` and `crn 8` read quietly, and every arm under
`crm 1` is quiet in both directions.

## §2 — WHAT I ACTUALLY NEED, AND IT IS NOT THE FIX

This project will not change what it cannot reproduce, and its rule is that a reproduction must run a
real guest instruction through real dispatch on a **committed, unmodified** machine description —
adding a machine or a device in order to reach a site is disqualifying.

I have no reproduction and I do not know whether one is possible. **That is the question.**

1. **Which in-tree machines install this handler**, and how? I believe `machine_iq80321.c` assigns it
   explicitly and that Iyonix does something similar — check both, and say whether any other machine
   reaches it.
2. **Can an ordinary guest instruction reach `arm_coproc_i80321_6` with `crm=0, crn=0, l_bit=1`?**
   Trace the path from instruction decode to this function. Does the dispatcher impose a privilege or
   mode precondition of its own, and is there any condition under which the coprocessor is absent or
   the access is refused before arriving here?
3. **Is there an existing vehicle in this tree that could carry such a test?** I know of
   `regress/`'s cold-debugger probes, which drive guest loads and stores through a debugger, and of an
   ARM decode gate. **Does anything in `regress/*.py` already execute an ARM coprocessor instruction,
   or drive an ARM guest at all?** Name what exists; if the answer is that the machinery is
   store/load-oriented and nothing drives a coprocessor transfer, say that plainly — a clear negative
   is exactly as useful to me as a positive here.
4. **What would the oracle be?** If the register is written and then read back, what distinguishes a
   correct implementation from (a) one that returns a constant zero, (b) one that returns a stale
   value, and (c) one that never executed the instruction at all? I want to know which of those a
   naive readback assertion would fail to separate.
5. **Which of the other terminating calls in this function would the same vehicle reach**, if one
   exists? I am not asking you to design their repairs — only whether one test vehicle covers several
   sites, because that changes how these are grouped into changes.
6. Here are four candidate readings of the proposed change. For each, say whether it is EQUIVALENT to
   deleting the two lines, or observably different, and name the first case where they differ:
   * (a) delete `exit(1)` and keep the `fatal(...)` line;
   * (b) delete both lines and also move the assignment after the `if`/`else`;
   * (c) replace both lines with a single bounded once-only diagnostic;
   * (d) delete both lines from `crn 0` and from `crn 8`'s write arm together.

Answer in full. Do not write any file; return the whole answer and I will save it.

