arm_endian_probe.py:28:
arm_endian_probe.py:29:Measured before the fix on -E barearm: stmia of r1=0x11223344,r2=0x55667788 to
arm_endian_probe.py:30:a WARM page laid down 44 33 22 11 88 77 66 55; DDI 0100I Table A2-2 (p. A2-32,
arm_endian_probe.py:31:with A2.7.2 p. A2-33 making LDM/STM a series of ordinary word accesses)
arm_endian_probe.py:32:requires 11 22 33 44 55 66 77 88. After the fix: the architectural bytes.
arm_endian_probe.py:33:
arm_endian_probe.py:34:THIS PROBE RUNS BOTH BYTE ORDERS.
arm_endian_probe.py:35:  * The BE rig is `-E barearm`, whose MACHINE_SETUP sets EMUL_BIG_ENDIAN
arm_endian_probe.py:36:    outright (machine_test.c) -- no config file, no ELF, no stub-order wrinkle.
arm_endian_probe.py:37:  * The LE rig is plain `-E testarm`. On LE the broken code was ACCIDENTALLY
arm_endian_probe.py:38:    RIGHT (host order == guest order), so LE rows discriminate NOTHING: they
arm_endian_probe.py:39:    are INVARIANCE CONTROLS, present to catch a fix that repairs BE by
arm_endian_probe.py:40:    breaking LE.
arm_endian_probe.py:41:
arm_endian_probe.py:42:Every row carries an EXPLICIT kind (DISC/CTRL) -- #372's rows derived kind from
arm_endian_probe.py:43:"cold in name", which #378's inverted polarity would have turned into a lie.
arm_endian_probe.py:44:
arm_endian_probe.py:45:The ldrb witnesses are the purest form, because a byte load has no byte order
arm_endian_probe.py:46:(data[0] in both branches), so they expose the raw memory layout with no
arm_endian_probe.py:47:dependence on any word path; each `buggy` column is the exact reverse of its
arm_endian_probe.py:48:`arch` column, so the mirror-image BE and LE groups cannot both be satisfied
arm_endian_probe.py:49:by a swapped expectation table. r10/r11 are printed as `sl`/`fp` -- the
arm_endian_probe.py:50:debugger's ARM_REG_NAMES have no r10/r11 spellings.
arm_endian_probe.py:51:
arm_endian_probe.py:52:In the STORE-test programs r1 = 0x11223344 and r2 = 0x55667788 are built with
arm_endian_probe.py:53:immediates, never loaded (a load would add its own path under test); the LDM
arm_endian_probe.py:54:programs of course DO load them -- that is what they test -- so those rows
arm_endian_probe.py:55:zero both registers first as sentinels.
arm_endian_probe.py:56:WARM pages are seeded with an explicit-width `put w` (which maps AND warms:
arm_endian_probe.py:57:MMU off => ok-1 == 1 => host_store too; the polarity is proven from source --
arm_endian_probe.py:58:memory_rw gates update_translation_table on !no_exceptions, and `put b` passes
arm_endian_probe.py:59:NO_EXCEPTIONS). Exact bytes on a warm page are laid with `put b` AFTER the
arm_endian_probe.py:60:`put w` (a byte write has no order and does not un-warm). The cold LDM page
arm_endian_probe.py:61:sees ONLY `put b`; the cold STM page is fully UNSEEDED (no access of any kind
arm_endian_probe.py:62:-- unseeded RAM is zero); #372's cold rows stay fully unseeded as before. The
arm_endian_probe.py:63:`put` width is spelled on EVERY command -- put_type is static in
arm_endian_probe.py:64:debugger_cmds.c and silently persists. #379: every put's echo is checked for
arm_endian_probe.py:65:the debugger's FAILED marker (PUT_STATUS) -- a silently failing `put w` would
arm_endian_probe.py:66:leave the warm pages cold and flip every warm DISC row to a FALSE GREEN on a
arm_endian_probe.py:67:buggy build, the one direction the controls must never allow.
arm_endian_probe.py:68:
arm_endian_probe.py:69:Every encoding below was verified through the emulator's own `unassemble`,
arm_endian_probe.py:70:CHECKING THE REGISTER FIELDS, not just the mnemonic (the round-117 lesson).
arm_endian_probe.py:71:"""
arm_endian_probe.py:72:import os
arm_endian_probe.py:73:import pty
arm_endian_probe.py:74:import re
arm_endian_probe.py:75:import select
arm_endian_probe.py:76:import sys
arm_endian_probe.py:77:import time
arm_endian_probe.py:78:
arm_endian_probe.py:79:BIN = sys.argv[1]
arm_endian_probe.py:80:CODE = 0x8000
arm_endian_probe.py:81:WARM = 0x10000
arm_endian_probe.py:82:COLD = 0x20000
arm_endian_probe.py:83:
arm_endian_probe.py:84:#  Build r1 = 0x11223344 with immediates (mov + 3 orr), verified via unassemble.
arm_endian_probe.py:85:BUILD_R1 = [0xE3A01411, 0xE3811822, 0xE3811C33, 0xE3811044]
arm_endian_probe.py:86:#  Build r2 = 0x55667788 (mov + 3 orr, Rd=Rn=2), #378.
arm_endian_probe.py:87:BUILD_R2 = [0xE3A02455, 0xE3822866, 0xE3822C77, 0xE3822088]
arm_endian_probe.py:88:MOV_R0_WARM = 0xE3A00801        # mov r0,#0x10000
arm_endian_probe.py:89:MOV_R0_COLD = 0xE3A00802        # mov r0,#0x20000
arm_endian_probe.py:90:MOV_R3_WARM = 0xE3A03801        # mov r3,#0x10000
arm_endian_probe.py:91:MOV_R3_COLD = 0xE3A03802        # mov r3,#0x20000
arm_endian_probe.py:92:STR_R1_R0 = 0xE5801000          # str  r1,[r0]
arm_endian_probe.py:93:LDR_R2_R0 = 0xE5902000          # ldr  r2,[r0]
arm_endian_probe.py:94:LDRB = (0xE5D03000, 0xE5D04001, 0xE5D05002, 0xE5D06003)  # ldrb r3..r6,[r0,#0..3]
arm_endian_probe.py:95:#  #378: stmia/ldmia r3,{r1,r2} (W=0 so r3 survives as the readback base) and
arm_endian_probe.py:96:#  an 8-byte ldrb ladder BASED ON R3 into r4..r11 (never clobbering r1/r2/r3).
arm_endian_probe.py:97:STMIA_R3 = 0xE8830006           # stmia r3,{r1,r2}
arm_endian_probe.py:98:LDMIA_R3 = 0xE8930006           # ldmia r3,{r1,r2}
arm_endian_probe.py:99:LDRB8_R3 = (0xE5D34000, 0xE5D35001, 0xE5D36002, 0xE5D37003,
arm_endian_probe.py:100:            0xE5D38004, 0xE5D39005, 0xE5D3A006, 0xE5D3B007)
arm_endian_probe.py:101:MOV_R1_0 = 0xE3A01000           # mov r1,#0  (LDM sentinel: a dead LDM reads 0)
arm_endian_probe.py:102:MOV_R2_0 = 0xE3A02000           # mov r2,#0
arm_endian_probe.py:103:SPIN = 0xEAFFFFFE               # b .
arm_endian_probe.py:104:
arm_endian_probe.py:105:#  #386: swp/swpb. X(swp) goes through memory_rw directly (no warm/cold fast
arm_endian_probe.py:106:#  path), so a put b-seeded page suffices and there is no #372-style split.
arm_endian_probe.py:107:SWP_R2_R1_R0 = 0xE1002091      # swp  r2,r1,[r0]
arm_endian_probe.py:108:SWPB_R2_R1_R0 = 0xE1402091     # swpb r2,r1,[r0]  (bit 22 = B)
arm_endian_probe.py:109:ADD_R0_1 = 0xE2800001          # add r0,r0,#1   (unaligned rows)
arm_endian_probe.py:110:SUB_R0_1 = 0xE2400001          # sub r0,r0,#1   (ladder re-bases at aligned P)
arm_endian_probe.py:111:ADD_R0_2 = 0xE2800002          # add r0,r0,#2   (#387: the +2 offset groups)
arm_endian_probe.py:112:SUB_R0_2 = 0xE2400002          # sub r0,r0,#2
arm_endian_probe.py:113:LDRB_R7_R0_4 = 0xE5D07004      # ldrb r7,[r0,#4] -- the P+4 survivor sentinel
arm_endian_probe.py:114:BUILD_R1_5567 = [0xE3A01455,   # mov r1,#0x55000000
arm_endian_probe.py:115:                 0xE3811866,   # orr r1,r1,#0x00660000
arm_endian_probe.py:116:                 0xE3811C77,   # orr r1,r1,#0x00007700
arm_endian_probe.py:117:                 0xE3811088]   # orr r1,r1,#0x88      -> r1 = 0x55667788
arm_endian_probe.py:118:
arm_endian_probe.py:119:
arm_endian_probe.py:120:def store_prog(base_mov):
arm_endian_probe.py:121:    return BUILD_R1 + [base_mov, STR_R1_R0, LDR_R2_R0] + list(LDRB) + [SPIN]
arm_endian_probe.py:122:
arm_endian_probe.py:123:
arm_endian_probe.py:124:def stm_prog(base_mov):
arm_endian_probe.py:125:    return (BUILD_R1 + BUILD_R2 + [base_mov, STMIA_R3] + list(LDRB8_R3)
arm_endian_probe.py:126:            + [SPIN])
arm_endian_probe.py:127:
arm_endian_probe.py:128:
arm_endian_probe.py:129:def ldm_prog(base_mov):
arm_endian_probe.py:130:    return [MOV_R1_0, MOV_R2_0, base_mov, LDMIA_R3, SPIN]
arm_endian_probe.py:131:
arm_endian_probe.py:132:
arm_endian_probe.py:133:def seed_bytes(base):
arm_endian_probe.py:134:    """Explicit-width put b commands laying 11 22 33 44 55 66 77 88."""
arm_endian_probe.py:135:    return ["put b 0x%x, 0x%02x" % (base + i, v)
arm_endian_probe.py:136:            for i, v in enumerate((0x11, 0x22, 0x33, 0x44,
arm_endian_probe.py:137:                                   0x55, 0x66, 0x77, 0x88))]
arm_endian_probe.py:138:
arm_endian_probe.py:139:
arm_endian_probe.py:140:#  #390: `str rX,[pc,#imm]` used a base of instruction+12 where A5.2.2 (and
arm_endian_probe.py:141:#  A5.2.3/A5.2.4, and A5.3.2/A5.3.3 for mode 3) define R15-as-Rn to be the
arm_endian_probe.py:142:#  address of the instruction PLUS EIGHT.  One scratch word was serving two
arm_endian_probe.py:143:#  roles -- the BASE when Rn == PC, and the DATA when Rd == PC -- and the store
arm_endian_probe.py:144:#  arm set it to +12 for both.  LDRD was caught in it too: mode 3 encodes LDRD
arm_endian_probe.py:145:#  with L == 0, so the generator never defines A__L for it and it takes the
arm_endian_probe.py:146:#  store arm despite being a load.
arm_endian_probe.py:147:#
arm_endian_probe.py:148:#  Readback is ALWAYS through a register base, never a pc-relative load: the
arm_endian_probe.py:149:#  decoder folds pc-relative LOADS into a constant MOV at TRANSLATE time, so
arm_endian_probe.py:150:#  such a load reports the bytes as they were BEFORE the store under test.
arm_endian_probe.py:151:STR_R4_PC_40  = 0xE58F4040      # str r4,[pc,#0x40]
arm_endian_probe.py:152:STR_PC_R7     = 0xE587F000      # str pc,[r7]
arm_endian_probe.py:153:STR_PC_PC_40  = 0xE58FF040      # str pc,[pc,#0x40]
arm_endian_probe.py:154:LDRD_R0_PC_40 = 0xE1CF04D0      # ldrd r0,[pc,#0x40]
arm_endian_probe.py:155:MOV_R7_20000  = 0xE3A07802      # mov r7,#0x20000
arm_endian_probe.py:156:MOV_R3_8000   = 0xE3A03C80      # mov r3,#0x8000   (0x80 ror 24)
arm_endian_probe.py:157:ORR_R3_58     = 0xE3833058      # orr r3,r3,#0x58  -> r3 = 0x8058
arm_endian_probe.py:158:ORR_R3_48     = 0xE3833048      # orr r3,r3,#0x48  -> r3 = 0x8048
arm_endian_probe.py:159:LDR_R5_R3     = 0xE5935000      # ldr r5,[r3]
arm_endian_probe.py:160:LDR_R6_R3_4   = 0xE5936004      # ldr r6,[r3,#4]
arm_endian_probe.py:161:LDR_R5_R7     = 0xE5975000      # ldr r5,[r7]
arm_endian_probe.py:162:
arm_endian_probe.py:163:
arm_endian_probe.py:164:def str_base_prog():
arm_endian_probe.py:165:    """R1: str r4,[pc,#0x40] at CODE+0x10; read BOTH candidate words.
arm_endian_probe.py:166:
arm_endian_probe.py:167:    The +8 candidate is 0x8058 and the +12 candidate 0x805c, so one ldr pair
arm_endian_probe.py:168:    covers both and the WRONG candidate is asserted to hold its sentinel --
arm_endian_probe.py:169:    a row that only checked the right address would pass a store-to-both.
arm_endian_probe.py:170:    """
arm_endian_probe.py:171:    return (list(BUILD_R4) + [STR_R4_PC_40, MOV_R3_8000, ORR_R3_58,
arm_endian_probe.py:172:                              LDR_R5_R3, LDR_R6_R3_4, SPIN])
arm_endian_probe.py:173:
arm_endian_probe.py:174:
arm_endian_probe.py:175:def str_data_prog():
arm_endian_probe.py:176:    """R2: str pc,[r7] -- the DATA role, with an ordinary base register.
arm_endian_probe.py:177:
arm_endian_probe.py:178:    Must read instruction+12 BEFORE and AFTER the fix.  This is the row that
arm_endian_probe.py:179:    catches a role-aware guard written without an else: that leaves the scratch
arm_endian_probe.py:180:    word holding whatever the previous PC-relative instruction left in it.
arm_endian_probe.py:181:    """
arm_endian_probe.py:182:    return [MOV_R7_20000, STR_PC_R7, LDR_R5_R7, SPIN]
arm_endian_probe.py:183:
arm_endian_probe.py:184:
arm_endian_probe.py:185:def str_bothpc_prog():
arm_flags_probe.py:1:#!/usr/bin/env python3
arm_flags_probe.py:2:"""Gate 14's measuring instrument: ARM subtract-carry flags and undefined routing.
arm_flags_probe.py:3:
arm_flags_probe.py:4:Prints one line per row:  <name> <got> <want> ok|FAIL
arm_flags_probe.py:5:then ARM_FLAGS_CONTROL=OK|DEAD and ARM_FLAGS_RESULT=<passed>/<total>.
arm_flags_probe.py:6:gate_arm.sh does the asserting.
arm_flags_probe.py:7:
arm_flags_probe.py:8:How it measures
arm_flags_probe.py:9:---------------
arm_flags_probe.py:10:Cold debugger on `testarm` (SA1110), RAM at 0. The machine needs A file to
arm_flags_probe.py:11:construct -- it prints usage and exits without one -- so a four-byte raw stub is
arm_flags_probe.py:12:loaded at the code address purely to satisfy that; it is overwritten by every
arm_flags_probe.py:13:row before anything executes. LITTLE-endian, so `dump` renders bytes in memory
arm_flags_probe.py:14:order and every word read here is byte-swapped back (the PowerPC rig is the
arm_flags_probe.py:15:opposite case, and mixing the two up silently transposes every answer).
arm_flags_probe.py:16:
arm_flags_probe.py:17:The flags are read by the guest's OWN `mrs r3,cpsr`, never by a debugger
arm_flags_probe.py:18:register print: `mrs` is the architectural read, and it is the same path a real
arm_flags_probe.py:19:kernel uses. The result register is stored alongside, because these two defects
arm_flags_probe.py:20:are FLAG defects -- the computed value was already right -- and a row that
arm_flags_probe.py:21:checked only the flag could not tell a fixed flag from a broken result.
arm_flags_probe.py:22:
arm_flags_probe.py:23:The carry contract (ARM ARM, DDI 0100, subtract family)
arm_flags_probe.py:24:-------------------------------------------------------
arm_flags_probe.py:25:For SUB/CMP/RSB the carry-out is NOT-BORROW: set iff the 32-bit subtraction did
arm_flags_probe.py:26:not borrow. For SBC/RSC the operation is `a - b - NOT(C)` and the carry-out must
arm_flags_probe.py:27:account for that borrow-in term.
arm_flags_probe.py:28:
arm_flags_probe.py:29:#311 computed the flag as `a >= b` for all five. That is correct for the three
arm_flags_probe.py:30:that have no borrow-in, and WRONG for SBC/RSC exactly when `a == b` and the
arm_flags_probe.py:31:carry-in is clear: `a - a - 1` borrows, so carry must be CLEAR, and `a >= b`
arm_flags_probe.py:32:reports it SET. The fix computes the flag from the full 64-bit result that the
arm_flags_probe.py:33:file already has in hand (`c32 == c64`), which is the same answer as `a >= b`
arm_flags_probe.py:34:whenever there is no borrow-in -- hence the many PIN rows here, which exist to
arm_flags_probe.py:35:prove the fix did NOT disturb the three instructions that were already right.
arm_flags_probe.py:36:
arm_flags_probe.py:37:The carry-in for each row is established by a plain SUBS, never by a debugger
arm_flags_probe.py:38:write to cpsr: a debugger write sets cpsr but not the separate `flags` field the
arm_flags_probe.py:39:dyntrans handlers actually read, so it would preset nothing. SUBS is the correct
arm_flags_probe.py:40:choice because it is the one member of the family whose carry both the committed
arm_flags_probe.py:41:build and the fix compute identically -- the preset cannot itself be the bug.
arm_flags_probe.py:42:
arm_flags_probe.py:43:The undefined contract
arm_flags_probe.py:44:----------------------
arm_flags_probe.py:45:#312: ARM's permanently-undefined space must raise the Undefined Instruction
arm_flags_probe.py:46:exception. The committed build reached `goto bad`, which sets cpu->running = 0
arm_flags_probe.py:47:(cpu_dyntrans.c:2014) -- a legal-to-attempt encoding stopping the whole
arm_flags_probe.py:48:emulator, the same halt class as #264/#309/#310. GDB's breakpoint instruction
arm_flags_probe.py:49:lives in this space, which is why the tree already contained an `und` routing
arm_flags_probe.py:50:for it at cpu_arm_instr.c -- unreachable, because the block above it matches the
arm_flags_probe.py:51:identical encodings first and breaks.
arm_flags_probe.py:52:
arm_flags_probe.py:53:The witness is the HANDLER, not the absence of a message: code planted at the
arm_flags_probe.py:54:UND vector (0x04) stores a marker, the mode, and the return address. A row that
arm_flags_probe.py:55:merely checked "the emulator did not halt" would pass if the instruction were
arm_flags_probe.py:56:silently treated as a nop.
arm_flags_probe.py:57:
arm_flags_probe.py:58:Row classes
arm_flags_probe.py:59:-----------
arm_flags_probe.py:60:  PIN  -- correct today and must stay. Most of this table is pins, on purpose:
arm_flags_probe.py:61:          the fix rewrites a line shared by five instructions and two of them
arm_flags_probe.py:62:          are the ones that were already right.
arm_flags_probe.py:63:  DISC -- discriminator: the committed byte is the measured defect, and the
arm_flags_probe.py:64:          want flips when the fix lands.
arm_flags_probe.py:65:"""
arm_flags_probe.py:66:import os
arm_flags_probe.py:67:import pty
arm_flags_probe.py:68:import re
arm_flags_probe.py:69:import select
arm_flags_probe.py:70:import sys
arm_flags_probe.py:71:import time
arm_flags_probe.py:72:
arm_flags_probe.py:73:BIN = sys.argv[1]
arm_flags_probe.py:74:STUB = sys.argv[2]
arm_flags_probe.py:75:
arm_flags_probe.py:76:CODE = 0x8000
arm_flags_probe.py:77:DEST = 0x9000
arm_flags_probe.py:78:VECT = 0x0004                   # ARM_EXCEPTION_UND * 4, low vectors
arm_flags_probe.py:79:#  Last instruction slot of a 4KB ic page. Deliberately NOT the page holding
arm_flags_probe.py:80:#  CODE: there, pc+4 would land exactly on DEST and the expected lr would be
arm_flags_probe.py:81:#  numerically equal to the witness address, which reads as a coincidence
arm_flags_probe.py:82:#  rather than a measurement.
arm_flags_probe.py:83:PAGE_END = 0xAFFC
arm_flags_probe.py:84:
arm_flags_probe.py:85:#  Instruction words. Rd=r0, Rn=r1, Rm=r2 throughout; r3 carries cpsr.
arm_flags_probe.py:86:SUBS_R6_R6_R7 = 0xE0566007      # subs r6,r6,r7   -- the carry preset
arm_flags_probe.py:87:MRS_R3_CPSR   = 0xE10F3000      # mrs  r3,cpsr
arm_flags_probe.py:88:STR_R3_R4     = 0xE5843000      # str  r3,[r4]
arm_flags_probe.py:89:STR_R0_R4     = 0xE5840000      # str  r0,[r4]
arm_flags_probe.py:90:STR_R0_R5     = 0xE5850000      # str  r0,[r5]
arm_flags_probe.py:91:STR_LR_R5     = 0xE585E000      # str  r14,[r5]
arm_flags_probe.py:92:STR_R0_R6     = 0xE5860000      # str  r0,[r6]
arm_flags_probe.py:93:MOV_R0_5A     = 0xE3A0005A      # mov  r0,#0x5a
arm_flags_probe.py:94:NOP           = 0xE1A00000      # mov  r0,r0
arm_flags_probe.py:95:
arm_flags_probe.py:96:SBCS = 0xE0D10002               # sbcs r0,r1,r2
arm_flags_probe.py:97:RSCS = 0xE0F10002               # rscs r0,r1,r2   (rd = r2 - r1 - !C)
arm_flags_probe.py:98:SUBS = 0xE0510002               # subs r0,r1,r2
arm_flags_probe.py:99:RSBS = 0xE0710002               # rsbs r0,r1,r2   (rd = r2 - r1)
arm_flags_probe.py:100:CMPS = 0xE1510002               # cmp  r1,r2
arm_flags_probe.py:101:SUBS_PC_4 = 0xE25F0004          # subs r0,pc,#4   (the A__PC template variant)
arm_flags_probe.py:102:ADDS = 0xE0910002               # adds r0,r1,r2
arm_flags_probe.py:103:ADCS = 0xE0B10002               # adcs r0,r1,r2
arm_flags_probe.py:104:
arm_flags_probe.py:105:UDF        = 0xE7F000F0         # permanently undefined (UDF #0)
arm_flags_probe.py:106:UDF_GDB    = 0xE6000010         # the GDB-breakpoint form, same space
arm_flags_probe.py:107:UDF_EQ     = 0x07F000F0         # the same encoding, condition EQ
arm_flags_probe.py:108:ARM_MODE_UND32 = 0x1b
arm_flags_probe.py:109:
arm_flags_probe.py:110:
arm_flags_probe.py:111:def session(cmds, nwords):
arm_flags_probe.py:112:    """Run one cold-debugger session; return (words, transcript)."""
arm_flags_probe.py:113:    #  -A disables colorized output. Under pty.fork() both isatty() checks
arm_flags_probe.py:114:    #  pass, so a CLICOLOR in the caller's environment would otherwise put
arm_flags_probe.py:115:    #  ANSI escapes between "cpu " and the debugmsg name and silently fail
arm_flags_probe.py:116:    #  every marker grep -- an environment-dependent FAIL that looks like a
arm_flags_probe.py:117:    #  real regression (a round-99 review seat's catch).
arm_flags_probe.py:118:    pid, fd = pty.fork()
arm_flags_probe.py:119:    if pid == 0:
arm_flags_probe.py:120:        os.execvp(BIN, [BIN, "-V", "-A", "-E", "testarm", "-M", "64",
arm_flags_probe.py:121:                        "0x%x:%s" % (CODE, STUB)])
arm_flags_probe.py:122:        os._exit(127)
arm_flags_probe.py:123:    buf = ""
arm_flags_probe.py:124:
arm_flags_probe.py:125:    def rd(t=0.4):
arm_flags_probe.py:126:        nonlocal buf
arm_flags_probe.py:127:        r, _, _ = select.select([fd], [], [], t)
arm_flags_probe.py:128:        if fd not in r:
arm_flags_probe.py:129:            return True
arm_flags_probe.py:130:        try:
arm_flags_probe.py:131:            d = os.read(fd, 65536)
arm_flags_probe.py:132:        except OSError:
arm_flags_probe.py:133:            return False
arm_flags_probe.py:134:        if not d:
arm_flags_probe.py:135:            return False
arm_flags_probe.py:136:        buf += d.decode("latin1", "replace")
arm_flags_probe.py:137:        return True
arm_flags_probe.py:138:
arm_flags_probe.py:139:    def wait(timeout=45):
arm_flags_probe.py:140:        t = time.time()
arm_flags_probe.py:141:        while time.time() - t < timeout:
arm_flags_probe.py:142:            if not rd():
arm_flags_probe.py:143:                return False
arm_flags_probe.py:144:            if buf.rstrip().endswith("GXemul>"):
arm_flags_probe.py:145:                return True
arm_flags_probe.py:146:        return False
arm_flags_probe.py:147:
arm_flags_probe.py:148:    def send(s):
arm_flags_probe.py:149:        b = (s + "\n").encode("latin1")
arm_flags_probe.py:150:        n = 0
arm_flags_probe.py:151:        while n < len(b):
arm_flags_probe.py:152:            n += os.write(fd, b[n:])
arm_flags_probe.py:153:        wait()
arm_flags_probe.py:154:
arm_flags_probe.py:155:    if not wait(60):
arm_flags_probe.py:156:        try:
arm_flags_probe.py:157:            os.kill(pid, 9)
arm_flags_probe.py:158:            os.waitpid(pid, 0)
arm_flags_probe.py:159:        except Exception:
arm_flags_probe.py:160:            pass
arm_flags_probe.py:161:        return None, buf
arm_flags_probe.py:162:
arm_flags_probe.py:163:    for c in cmds:
arm_flags_probe.py:164:        send(c)
arm_flags_probe.py:165:
arm_flags_probe.py:166:    #  Retry the READ, never the row: a dump can straggle past the prompt
arm_flags_probe.py:167:    #  detector under host load, and the value cannot change between dumps.
arm_flags_probe.py:168:    #  A row that lost this retry showed up as a spurious DEAD in the very
arm_flags_probe.py:169:    #  first baseline sweep.
arm_flags_probe.py:170:    flat = []
arm_flags_probe.py:171:    for _ in range(3):
arm_flags_probe.py:172:        mark = len(buf)
arm_flags_probe.py:173:        send("dump 0x%x 0x%x" % (DEST, DEST + 4 * nwords))
arm_flags_probe.py:174:        words = re.findall(r"0x0*[0-9a-f]+\s+((?:[0-9a-f]{8}\s+){1,4})",
arm_flags_probe.py:175:                           buf[mark:])
arm_flags_probe.py:176:        flat = "".join(words).split()
arm_flags_probe.py:177:        if len(flat) >= nwords:
arm_flags_probe.py:178:            break
arm_flags_probe.py:179:        time.sleep(1.0)
arm_flags_probe.py:180:        rd(1.0)
arm_flags_probe.py:181:    try:
arm_flags_probe.py:182:        os.write(fd, b"quit\n")
arm_flags_probe.py:183:        time.sleep(0.3)
arm_flags_probe.py:184:        os.kill(pid, 9)
arm_flags_probe.py:185:        os.waitpid(pid, 0)
footbridge_sites_probe.py:1:#!/usr/bin/env python3
footbridge_sites_probe.py:2:"""#432: the DC21285 "footbridge" driven by REAL GUEST INSTRUCTIONS, with no rig.
footbridge_sites_probe.py:3:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:4:WHY THIS EXISTS ALONGSIDE regress/diff_footbridge.c.  The offline differential calls
footbridge_sites_probe.py:5:footbridge_effective_cycles() and dev_footbridge_access() directly, so it proves BEHAVIOUR
footbridge_sites_probe.py:6:but cannot prove the device is REACHABLE by a guest -- it would stay green under a change
footbridge_sites_probe.py:7:that unmapped it (a memory_device_register length, a DM_ flag, or machine_cats.c's
footbridge_sites_probe.py:8:`addr=0x42000000` line).  Until this probe, R6 was THE ONLY CODE ROUND IN THIS TREE WITH NO
footbridge_sites_probe.py:9:REACHABILITY WITNESS OF ANY KIND: no rig boots a footbridge and there is no m8online analog.
footbridge_sites_probe.py:10:This is that analog.  It goes through the real address decode and the real memory_rw
footbridge_sites_probe.py:11:plumbing, on a COLD DEBUGGER -- no boot, no disk image, no guest OS.
footbridge_sites_probe.py:12:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:13:*** THE TRAP THIS PROBE EXISTS TO SURVIVE, and it is the whole reason for arm T. ***
footbridge_sites_probe.py:14:machine_cats.c:131 calls arm_setup_initial_translation_table(), which (cpu_arm.c:239-255)
footbridge_sites_probe.py:15:writes a section descriptor for i in 0..255 at VA (j<<28)+(i<<20) -> PA i<<20 -- i.e. the
footbridge_sites_probe.py:16:TOP NIBBLE OF THE VA IS IGNORED and only the low 256 MB of physical space is mapped
footbridge_sites_probe.py:17:anywhere.  The footbridge sits at PA 0x42000000, outside that window, so under the boot-time
footbridge_sites_probe.py:18:MMU it is UNREACHABLE: VA 0x42000000 aliases to PA 0x02000000 and a load returns 0.
footbridge_sites_probe.py:19:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:20:That is not a hypothetical.  The first version of this probe returned 0x0 on every device
footbridge_sites_probe.py:21:row WITH ITS RAM CONTROL STILL GREEN, and 0x0 is exactly what "the device is not there"
footbridge_sites_probe.py:22:looks like -- so a survival-shaped or absence-shaped assertion would have CONFIRMED THE
footbridge_sites_probe.py:23:OPPOSITE CONCLUSION.  A RAM liveness row cannot see it, because VA 0x9000 maps to PA 0x9000
footbridge_sites_probe.py:24:identically with the MMU on or off.
footbridge_sites_probe.py:25:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:26:So the guard is not a comment, it is arm T: THE SAME PROGRAM WITH THE THREE MMU-DISABLE
footbridge_sites_probe.py:27:INSTRUCTIONS REPLACED BY NOPS MUST NOT PRODUCE THE DEVICE'S ANSWER.  If arm T ever reads
footbridge_sites_probe.py:28:0x1011, the discriminator is dead and every other row here is meaningless -- and it goes RED
footbridge_sites_probe.py:29:rather than silently green.  Arm L additionally reads CP15 c1 BACK after the mcr and asserts
footbridge_sites_probe.py:30:bit 0 is clear, so the precondition is MEASURED rather than inferred from the store having
footbridge_sites_probe.py:31:been issued.  Three separate statements, deliberately: the guest turned the MMU off, the two
footbridge_sites_probe.py:32:arms really differ in MMU state, and only the MMU-off arm can see the device.
footbridge_sites_probe.py:33:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:34:VALUES, NOT SURVIVAL (rounds 79/80, and m8820x_sites_probe.py's header says the same).
footbridge_sites_probe.py:35:A row that only asks "did the host stay up" cannot tell a reachable device from an absent
footbridge_sites_probe.py:36:one -- an unmapped read survives too, it just returns 0.  So the two device rows assert
footbridge_sites_probe.py:37:values that ONLY dev_footbridge.c can produce:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:38:  * A1 reads VENDOR_ID and requires 0x1011 (dev_footbridge.c:423, DC21285_VENDOR_ID).
footbridge_sites_probe.py:39:    Nonzero and specific: the hand-assembled-encoding trap this project has been bitten by
footbridge_sites_probe.py:40:    yields 0 from a wrong register field, and 0 is the one answer this row rejects.
footbridge_sites_probe.py:41:  * A2 writes 0x12345678 to TIMER_1_LOAD and reads back 0x00345678.  THE VANISHING TOP BYTE
footbridge_sites_probe.py:42:    IS THE HANDLER'S OWN SIGNATURE: dev_footbridge.c:548 stores `idata & TIMER_MAX_VAL`
footbridge_sites_probe.py:43:    (0x00FFFFFF, dc21285reg.h:371).  RAM, or any decode that merely absorbed the store,
footbridge_sites_probe.py:44:    would return 0x12345678 unchanged.  This row therefore distinguishes "the device
footbridge_sites_probe.py:45:    handled it" from "the write landed somewhere", which A1 alone cannot.
footbridge_sites_probe.py:46:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:47:THE KILL PAIR, which is this probe's own failability control.  Arms L and K differ by
footbridge_sites_probe.py:48:EXACTLY ONE INSTRUCTION.  Arm K adds a guest read of IRQ_ENABLE_SET (base+0x188), which
footbridge_sites_probe.py:49:dev_footbridge.c:495-497 answers with fatal() then exit(1) -- an UNFIXED site, used here
footbridge_sites_probe.py:50:rather than repaired, because a probe that reports SURVIVED for every row is vacuous unless
footbridge_sites_probe.py:51:something in it can still die.  Arm L must live; arm K must exit 1 and say so.  If both
footbridge_sites_probe.py:52:survived, the guest instructions are not reaching the device at all and every SURVIVED
footbridge_sites_probe.py:53:verdict above is worthless; if both died, the probe is killing the host for its own reasons.
footbridge_sites_probe.py:54:The one-instruction delta is the entire argument.
footbridge_sites_probe.py:55:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:56:EVERY PLANTED WORD IS CHECKED AGAINST THE DISASSEMBLY THE EMULATOR PRINTS AS IT STEPS IT --
footbridge_sites_probe.py:57:not against a separate `unassemble`, because the step line is the instruction that actually
footbridge_sites_probe.py:58:executed.  This project has a recorded incident where a wrong register field made a gate row
footbridge_sites_probe.py:59:measure the wrong thing for months.
footbridge_sites_probe.py:60:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:61:COST, measured: see PROBE_WALL below -- 3 cold-debugger launches, no boot.  A booting-rig row
footbridge_sites_probe.py:62:was deliberately NOT written: no rig in this tree boots a footbridge machine at all, which is
footbridge_sites_probe.py:63:the gap this probe closes, and adding one would put a load-sensitive wall-clock oracle into a
footbridge_sites_probe.py:64:battery already carrying ~37.
footbridge_sites_probe.py:65:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:66:usage: footbridge_sites_probe.py <gxemul-binary> <four-byte-raw-stub>
footbridge_sites_probe.py:67:The stub is the same scaffolding gate_arm.sh writes for testarm: `cats` needs a file
footbridge_sites_probe.py:68:argument to construct, and every word of it is overwritten before anything executes.
footbridge_sites_probe.py:69:"""
footbridge_sites_probe.py:70:import os, pty, re, select, sys, time
footbridge_sites_probe.py:71:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:72:BIN = sys.argv[1]
footbridge_sites_probe.py:73:STUB = sys.argv[2]
footbridge_sites_probe.py:74:CODE = 0x00008000
footbridge_sites_probe.py:75:DATA = 0x00009000
footbridge_sites_probe.py:76:NOP = 0xE1A00000
footbridge_sites_probe.py:77:NOPTXT = "mov r0,r0"      #  the EMULATOR's spelling of 0xe1a00000; it does not print "nop"
footbridge_sites_probe.py:78:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:79:#  (offset, word, expected disassembly text, comment)
footbridge_sites_probe.py:80:#  Spellings are the EMULATOR's own, not a manual's: "mrc 15,0,r0,cr1,cr0,0" and decimal
footbridge_sites_probe.py:81:#  ldr/str offsets ("[r0,#768]" for 0x300) are what this disassembler prints.
footbridge_sites_probe.py:82:MMU_OFF = [
footbridge_sites_probe.py:83:    (0x00, 0xEE110F10, "mrc 15,0,r0,cr1,cr0,0", "read CP15 c1"),
footbridge_sites_probe.py:84:    (0x04, 0xE3C00001, "bic r0,r0,#1",          "clear the MMU enable bit"),
footbridge_sites_probe.py:85:    (0x08, 0xEE010F10, "mcr 15,0,r0,cr1,cr0,0", "MMU OFF -> VA == PA"),
footbridge_sites_probe.py:86:]
footbridge_sites_probe.py:87:MMU_NOP = [
footbridge_sites_probe.py:88:    (0x00, NOP, NOPTXT, "arm T: MMU LEFT ON -- the negative control"),
footbridge_sites_probe.py:89:    (0x04, NOP, NOPTXT, ""),
footbridge_sites_probe.py:90:    (0x08, NOP, NOPTXT, ""),
footbridge_sites_probe.py:91:]
footbridge_sites_probe.py:92:BODY = [
footbridge_sites_probe.py:93:    (0x0c, 0xEE113F10, "mrc 15,0,r3,cr1,cr0,0", "C2: read CP15 c1 BACK -- measures the state"),
footbridge_sites_probe.py:94:    (0x10, 0xE3A00442, "mov r0,#0x42000000",    "footbridge base (machine_cats.c:64)"),
footbridge_sites_probe.py:95:    (0x14, 0xE3A04A09, "mov r4,#0x9000",        "scratch RAM"),
footbridge_sites_probe.py:96:    (0x18, 0xE5945000, "ldr r5,[r4]",           "C1 liveness: plain RAM, 0x11223344"),
footbridge_sites_probe.py:97:    (0x1c, 0xE5906000, "ldr r6,[r0]",           "A1: VENDOR_ID"),
footbridge_sites_probe.py:98:    (0x20, 0xE5942004, "ldr r2,[r4,#4]",        "load 0x12345678 from RAM"),
footbridge_sites_probe.py:99:    (0x24, 0xE5802300, "str r2,[r0,#768]",      "A2: TIMER_1_LOAD <- 0x12345678"),
footbridge_sites_probe.py:100:    (0x28, 0xE5901300, "ldr r1,[r0,#768]",      "A2: read it back"),
footbridge_sites_probe.py:101:]
footbridge_sites_probe.py:102:KILL_NOP  = (0x2c, NOP,        NOPTXT,             "arm L: no kill site")
footbridge_sites_probe.py:103:KILL_LOAD = (0x2c, 0xE5907188, "ldr r7,[r0,#392]", "arm K: read IRQ_ENABLE_SET (0x188)")
footbridge_sites_probe.py:104:TAIL = [(0x30, NOP, NOPTXT, "landing pad -- planted, never stepped")]
footbridge_sites_probe.py:105:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:106:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:107:def run_arm(label, prog):
footbridge_sites_probe.py:108:    """Plant `prog`, step it one instruction at a time, report registers and liveness."""
footbridge_sites_probe.py:109:    pid, fd = pty.fork()
footbridge_sites_probe.py:110:    if pid == 0:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:111:        os.execvp(BIN, [BIN, "-V", "-T", "-E", "cats", "-M", "32",
footbridge_sites_probe.py:112:                        "0x%x:%s" % (CODE, STUB)])
footbridge_sites_probe.py:113:        os._exit(127)
footbridge_sites_probe.py:114:    buf = ""
footbridge_sites_probe.py:115:    dead = [False]
footbridge_sites_probe.py:116:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:117:    def rd(t=0.4):
footbridge_sites_probe.py:118:        nonlocal buf
footbridge_sites_probe.py:119:        r, _, _ = select.select([fd], [], [], t)
footbridge_sites_probe.py:120:        if fd not in r:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:121:            return True
footbridge_sites_probe.py:122:        try:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:123:            d = os.read(fd, 65536)
footbridge_sites_probe.py:124:        except OSError:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:125:            dead[0] = True
footbridge_sites_probe.py:126:            return False
footbridge_sites_probe.py:127:        if not d:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:128:            dead[0] = True
footbridge_sites_probe.py:129:            return False
footbridge_sites_probe.py:130:        buf += d.decode("latin1", "replace")
footbridge_sites_probe.py:131:        return True
footbridge_sites_probe.py:132:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:133:    #  Signature order is not free choice: gate_hygiene.sh pins the CANONICAL #392 call form
footbridge_sites_probe.py:134:    #  as a literal `return wait(mark=_mark, echo=<x> if <x> else None)`, tightened three
footbridge_sites_probe.py:135:    #  times because every looser prefix was a substring of the broken form too.  Written any
footbridge_sites_probe.py:136:    #  other way, a probe is simply not covered by that ratchet -- which is worse than failing
footbridge_sites_probe.py:137:    #  it.  So timeout goes last, and send() ends on the pinned form.
footbridge_sites_probe.py:138:    def wait(mark=0, echo=None, timeout=60):
footbridge_sites_probe.py:139:        t = time.time()
footbridge_sites_probe.py:140:        while time.time() - t < timeout:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:141:            if not rd():
footbridge_sites_probe.py:142:                return False
footbridge_sites_probe.py:143:            resp = buf[mark:]
footbridge_sites_probe.py:144:            if echo is not None and echo not in resp:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:145:                continue
footbridge_sites_probe.py:146:            if len(buf) > mark and resp.rstrip().endswith("GXemul>"):
footbridge_sites_probe.py:147:                return True
footbridge_sites_probe.py:148:        return False
footbridge_sites_probe.py:149:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:150:    def send(x, timeout=60):
footbridge_sites_probe.py:151:        b = (x + "\n").encode("latin1")
footbridge_sites_probe.py:152:        _mark = len(buf)
footbridge_sites_probe.py:153:        n = 0
footbridge_sites_probe.py:154:        try:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:155:            while n < len(b):
footbridge_sites_probe.py:156:                n += os.write(fd, b[n:])
footbridge_sites_probe.py:157:        except OSError:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:158:            dead[0] = True
footbridge_sites_probe.py:159:            return False
footbridge_sites_probe.py:160:        return wait(mark=_mark, echo=x if x else None)
footbridge_sites_probe.py:161:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:162:    status, regs, badis = "?", {}, []
footbridge_sites_probe.py:163:    #  KEYWORD, and this is not style.  A positional wait(120) silently becomes mark=120 under
footbridge_sites_probe.py:164:    #  the pinned parameter order -- a real behaviour change, and the exact mistake the
footbridge_sites_probe.py:165:    #  m8820x probe's conversion had to fix in the same commit.
footbridge_sites_probe.py:166:    if not wait(timeout=120):
footbridge_sites_probe.py:167:        status = "NO-PROMPT"
footbridge_sites_probe.py:168:    else:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:169:        send("put w 0x%08x, 0x%08x" % (DATA, 0x11223344))
footbridge_sites_probe.py:170:        send("put w 0x%08x, 0x%08x" % (DATA + 4, 0x12345678))
footbridge_sites_probe.py:171:        for off, word, _t, _c in prog:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:172:            send("put w 0x%08x, 0x%08x" % (CODE + off, word))
footbridge_sites_probe.py:173:        send("pc=0x%08x" % CODE)
footbridge_sites_probe.py:174:        status = "SURVIVED"
footbridge_sites_probe.py:175:        for off, word, text, _c in prog[:-1]:      # the landing pad is never stepped
footbridge_sites_probe.py:176:            mark = len(buf)
footbridge_sites_probe.py:177:            if not send("step 1", timeout=30) or dead[0]:
footbridge_sites_probe.py:178:                status = "HOST-DIED"
footbridge_sites_probe.py:179:                break
footbridge_sites_probe.py:180:            seg = buf[mark:]
footbridge_sites_probe.py:181:            #  Match the emulator's own step line for THIS address and compare the mnemonic
footbridge_sites_probe.py:182:            #  it printed against what this table says was planted.
footbridge_sites_probe.py:183:            m = re.search(r"%08x:\s+%08x\s+([^\r\n<]+)" % (CODE + off, word), seg)
footbridge_sites_probe.py:184:            got = re.sub(r"\s+", " ", m.group(1)).strip() if m else "(no step line)"
footbridge_sites_probe.py:185:            if got != text:

